361:## 6. Technology choices and alternatives considered
363:### Decision: What ExerciseDB/CDN will be used
370:### Decision: Which Backend as a Service (BaaS) technology will be used?
372:- **Chosen**: Supabase
374:- **Why the chosen option wins, given our goals**: 2 main reasons: First, Supabase is better build for open-source contributions, since contributors can run a local version of the backend without needing access to the production credentials. Second, the data model for LibreLift (workouts, exercises, template vs session) fits well in a relational model, which is more readily available in Supabase. One advantage that Firebase does have is out of the box support for offline-synchronization.
375:- **Evidence**: The Supabase CLI allows any developer to run backend services locally with one command (`npx supabase start`). This is a huge asset for an open-source project. Supabase is naturally relational with PostgreSQL, and Firebase is natively NoSQL. Firebase does support SQL using SQL Connect, which requires some additional configuration. Offline synchronization is possible with Supabase, but requires more manual configuration than Firebase.
376:- **What would change our mind**: If offline synchronization became too complex with Supabase, switching to Firebase would become a real consideration. Or, if the scale of the project grows enough and pricing between Supabase and Firebase favor Firebase, that would also merit considering a switch.
378:### Decision: What authentication method will be used
383:**Evidence**: Supabase supports email OTP for new and returning users and renewable sessions without a default maximum lifetime. During the Issue #42 design discussion, this approach was selected based on the expected infrequency of sign-ins for a gym app. The expectation is that users will sign in once and then use the app as desired.
